# Metagol: learn programs that invent, recurse, and compose

Source: [Metagol repository](https://github.com/metagol/metagol).

A few examples go in. A readable Prolog program comes out. In this lab you will
learn **a relation that needs an invented concept**, **an unbounded recursive
relation**, and **a transformation over nested lists**. Every program below is
learned by the actual Metagol engine when you evaluate the cell.

## 1. The learning contract

Inductive Logic Programming learns a hypothesis **H**, using background
knowledge **B**, positive examples **E+**, negative examples **E−**, and a
**language bias** that limits which programs may be proposed.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  B["Background: available predicates"] --> M["Metagol: search over metarules"]
  E["Examples: positive and negative"] --> M
  R["Bias: clause templates and budget"] --> M
  M --> H["Learned Prolog clauses"]
  H --> T["Execute on unseen queries"]
```

The training objective is to explain positives and reject negatives. Many
programs can fit a small dataset; what the learner can express and what we test
matter as much as training accuracy. A finite passing test suite is evidence,
not a proof that the learned concept is universally correct.

Set `SWI_ELIXIR_PATH` to this checkout's absolute path. Install SWI and compile
the NIF as described in the README. No downloads are needed for the learner:
the Prolog source is in `vendor/metagol/`.

```elixir
project = System.fetch_env!("SWI_ELIXIR_PATH")
Mix.install([{:swi_elixir, path: project}])
Code.require_file(Path.join(project, "examples/ilp.exs"))
backend = :port
```

```elixir
inline_tasks = %{
  "metagol_tasks.pl" => ~S"""
:- module(metagol_tasks, [primitives/2, training/3, tests/2]).

mg_mother(ann,amy). mg_mother(ann,andy). mg_mother(amy,amelia).
mg_mother(linda,gavin). mg_mother(nora,ivy).
mg_father(steve,amy). mg_father(steve,andy). mg_father(gavin,amelia).
mg_father(andy,spongebob). mg_father(ivy,leo).

mg_parent(queen_victoria,edward_seventh).
mg_parent(edward_seventh,george_fifth).
mg_parent(george_fifth,george_sixth).
mg_parent(george_sixth,elizabeth_second).
mg_parent(queen_mother,elizabeth_second).
mg_parent(elizabeth_second,prince_charles).
mg_parent(prince_philip,prince_charles).
mg_parent(prince_charles,prince_william).
mg_parent(prince_charles,prince_harry).
mg_parent(princess_diana,prince_william).
mg_parent(princess_diana,prince_harry).
mg_parent(prince_william,prince_george).

primitives(grandparent, [mg_mother/2,mg_father/2]).
primitives(ancestor, [mg_parent/2]).
primitives(droplasts, [mg_tail/2,mg_reverse/2]).

training(grandparent,
 [grandparent(ann,amelia),grandparent(steve,amelia),grandparent(ann,spongebob),
  grandparent(steve,spongebob),grandparent(linda,amelia)],
 [grandparent(amy,amelia)]).
training(ancestor,
 [ancestor(elizabeth_second,prince_charles),ancestor(george_sixth,prince_harry),
  ancestor(queen_mother,prince_william)], []).
training(droplasts,
 [droplasts([[a,b,c],[d,e,f,g]],[[a,b],[d,e,f]]),
  droplasts([[h,i],[j,k,l],[m,n,o,p]],[[h],[j,k],[m,n,o]])],
 [droplasts([[a,b,c]],[[b,c]])]).

tests(grandparent,
 [grandparent(nora,leo)-true, grandparent(ann,gavin)-false,
  grandparent(ann,ann)-false]).
tests(ancestor,
 [ancestor(queen_victoria,prince_george)-true,
  ancestor(princess_diana,prince_george)-true,
  ancestor(prince_george,queen_victoria)-false, ancestor(queen_victoria,queen_victoria)-false]).
tests(droplasts,
 [droplasts([[q],[r,s,t,u,v],[]],[[],[r,s,t,u],[]])-false,
  droplasts([[q],[r,s,t,u,v]],[[],[r,s,t,u]])-true,
  droplasts([],[])-true, droplasts([[x,y]],[[y]])-false]).
"""
}
```


Change `backend` to `:nif` and reevaluate to use the same tasks and API with
embedded SWI. The helper opens one session, runs one bounded learning experiment,
and stops in an `after` block. Upstream Metagol uses shared mutable registries;
these demonstrations serialize their learning calls and restore the settings
and primitive declarations they change. Run them serially alongside other
Metagol code in the same native VM. Port gives each session its own registry.

## 2. Invent the missing concept: grandparent

The task supplies `mother/2` and `father/2`, but **does not supply `parent/2`**.
Our primitives are named `mg_mother/2` and `mg_father/2` to avoid collisions.

Five positive examples include grandparent paths through either type of parent;
`grandparent(amy,amelia)` is negative: being a mother is not enough. Two extra
background facts, `mg_mother(nora,ivy)` and `mg_father(ivy,leo)`, support a
project-authored test whose label is withheld from training.

```prolog
mg_mother(ann,amy). mg_mother(ann,andy). mg_mother(amy,amelia).
mg_mother(linda,gavin). mg_mother(nora,ivy).
mg_father(steve,amy). mg_father(steve,andy). mg_father(gavin,amelia).
mg_father(andy,spongebob). mg_father(ivy,leo).

training(grandparent,
 [grandparent(ann,amelia), grandparent(steve,amelia),
  grandparent(ann,spongebob), grandparent(steve,spongebob),
  grandparent(linda,amelia)],
 [grandparent(amy,amelia)]).
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Nora["nora"] -->|mother| Ivy["ivy"]
  Ivy -->|father| Leo["leo"]
  Nora -. "grandparent? label withheld" .-> Leo
```

Metarules are clause templates, not answers. Our shared bias contains these
four forms; `P`, `Q`, `R`, and `F` stand for predicate symbols:

| Template | Allowed clause | Idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Identity | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,B).` | Reuse a primitive |
| Chain | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,C), R(C,B).` | Compose two relations |
| Tail recursion | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,C), P(C,B).` | Follow arbitrarily long paths |
| Higher-order | `P(A,B) :- Q(A,B,F).` | Learn the predicate passed to a combinator |

These templates are declared in Prolog as:

```prolog
metarule(identity, [P,Q], [P,A,B], [[Q,A,B]]).
metarule(chain, [P,Q,R], [P,A,B], [[Q,A,C],[R,C,B]]).
metarule(tailrec, [P,Q], [P,A,B], [[Q,A,C],[P,C,B]]).
metarule(tohigherorder, [P,Q,F], [P,A,B], [[Q,A,B,F]]).
```

Before running, consider why one generic grandparent clause cannot directly
name both `mother` and `father` in every possible combination. A reusable
invented helper can combine them.

```elixir
grandparent = ILPShowcase.learn(:metagol, :grandparent, 3, backend, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
{:atom, "learned"} = grandparent["Status"]
3 = grandparent["Clauses"]
IO.puts(grandparent["Program"])
ILPShowcase.scores(grandparent)
```

Read the emitted clauses. The invented predicate usually has a name such as
`grandparent_1`; its role is **parent**, even though we never gave that concept a
name or a definition. The displayed program is the learner's returned
hypothesis, converted by Metagol's own clause converter and executed as Prolog.
It is not an expected program pasted into the notebook.

```elixir
grandparent["Rows"]
```

The test cases include the unseen `grandparent(nora,leo)`, an unrelated pair,
and a self-pair. Training rows and test rows are labelled separately.

### Experiment: make the program budget too small

Metagol deepens its search by clause count. A smaller budget is a stronger
language restriction, not a request to train for fewer iterations.

```elixir
budget_results = for budget <- [2, 3] do
  result = ILPShowcase.learn(:metagol, :grandparent, budget, backend, inline_tasks)
  expected = if budget == 2, do: "no_hypothesis", else: "learned"
  {:atom, ^expected} = result["Status"]
  %{budget: budget, status: expected, clauses: result["Clauses"], search_trace: result["Trace"]}
end
budget_results
```

`no_hypothesis` here means the bounded search found no consistent program in
this configured space. A timeout is different: the wrapper raises `SWI.Error`
and the result is unknown. The two-clause failure is not a claim about every
possible definition of grandparent in every logic language.

## 3. Learn recursion: one rule, any number of generations

The task uses a small acyclic family graph and three positive ancestor queries.
It supplies `parent/2`, but no ancestor implementation. The primitive is named
`mg_parent/2` to avoid collisions.

```prolog
mg_parent(queen_victoria,edward_seventh).
mg_parent(edward_seventh,george_fifth).
mg_parent(george_fifth,george_sixth).
mg_parent(george_sixth,elizabeth_second).
mg_parent(queen_mother,elizabeth_second).
mg_parent(elizabeth_second,prince_charles).
mg_parent(prince_philip,prince_charles).
mg_parent(prince_charles,prince_william).
mg_parent(prince_charles,prince_harry).
mg_parent(princess_diana,prince_william).
mg_parent(princess_diana,prince_harry).
mg_parent(prince_william,prince_george).

training(ancestor,
 [ancestor(elizabeth_second,prince_charles),
  ancestor(george_sixth,prince_harry),
  ancestor(queen_mother,prince_william)], []).
```

A two-clause program must handle both a direct edge and a longer path. Our
project-authored tests include Queen Victoria to Prince George, a path longer
than any training example, plus reverse and self queries. These names are
historical identifiers in the example data; this is a toy graph, not a
complete genealogy.

```elixir
ancestor = ILPShowcase.learn(:metagol, :ancestor, 2, backend, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
2 = ancestor["Clauses"]
IO.puts(ancestor["Program"])
Enum.filter(ancestor["Rows"], &(&1.split == "test"))
```

Locate the base clause and the recursive clause. Each recursive step consumes
one edge of this acyclic graph. We test **strict ancestry**: a person is not
their own ancestor. Cycles would require different operational care; do not
assume naive recursive Prolog terminates on arbitrary cyclic graphs.

## 4. Learn a program inside a program: nested droplasts

This task combines predicate invention with **interpreted background
knowledge**: Metagol can inspect the definition of `map`, rather than treating
it as an opaque primitive.

We use the same task with a reduced, explicit bias: `mg_tail/2`,
`mg_reverse/2`, the four templates above, and interpreted `map`. Our two
positive training examples are fixed instead of randomly generated; we also
add one negative example. **No droplast implementation is available to the
learner.**

```prolog
training(droplasts,
 [droplasts([[a,b,c],[d,e,f,g]],[[a,b],[d,e,f]]),
  droplasts([[h,i],[j,k,l],[m,n,o,p]],[[h],[j,k],[m,n,o]])],
 [droplasts([[a,b,c]],[[b,c]])]).
```

| Input | Required output |
| --- | --- |
| `[[a,b,c],[d,e,f,g]]` | `[[a,b],[d,e,f]]` |
| `[[h,i],[j,k,l],[m,n,o,p]]` | `[[h],[j,k],[m,n,o]]` |

```elixir
droplasts = ILPShowcase.learn(:metagol, :droplasts, 3, backend, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
3 = droplasts["Clauses"]
IO.puts(droplasts["Program"])
Enum.filter(droplasts["Rows"], &(&1.split == "test"))
```

Trace the invented transformation on `[r,s,t,u,v]`: reverse the list, remove
its head, then reverse again. `map` applies that invented transformation to
each sublist. This composition removes the **last** element, not the first.

The tests deliberately include a singleton sublist, a longer unseen sublist,
an empty outer list, and a tempting wrong output. An empty **inner** list is
outside the drop-last domain: the learned transformation fails because
`mg_tail([],_)` fails. That behavior is checked, not silently replaced with a
handwritten special case.

## 5. Check the runtime and the backend contract

Each learning query records SWI inference counts and elapsed time. Counts
include orchestration and evaluation, and are not a benchmark of the learner
alone.

```elixir
%{
  inferences: droplasts["Inferences"],
  elapsed_us: droplasts["ElapsedUs"]
}
```

```elixir
for {task, budget} <- [grandparent: 3, ancestor: 2, droplasts: 3] do
  port = ILPShowcase.learn(:metagol, task, budget, :port, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
  nif = ILPShowcase.learn(:metagol, task, budget, :nif, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
  true = port["Rows"] == nif["Rows"]
  true = port["Clauses"] == nif["Clauses"]
  %{task: task, clauses: port["Clauses"], scores: ILPShowcase.scores(port)}
end
```

## 6. Take the learner apart

Try these experiments by changing the corresponding Prolog task or metarule
above, then reevaluate the relevant cell. The default notebook is checked automatically; experiments may
legitimately change the expected results.

1. Remove the tail-recursive template. Can the two-clause ancestor task still
   explain its longest training example?
2. Add `mg_droplast/2` as a primitive. How many clauses are now needed, and how
   much of the original learning problem have you supplied as background?
3. Remove the negative droplasts example. Which unseen cases still distinguish
   a correct transformation from one that merely fits the positives?
4. Replace the outer-map task with “drop the last row too.” Predict the extra
   composition before trying it.

The next notebook shows a different inductive bias: Aleph searches clauses
within a bottom clause, using mode declarations instead of metarules.
